## Phone calls

This agent answers phone calls over Daily PSTN. Daily's own infrastructure
carries the call and hands it to this deployed agent, so nothing here listens on
a public port and there is no webhook server of yours in the path.

### Buy a phone number

Numbers come from your Daily account, with your Daily API key. That is a
different key from the Pipecat Cloud one that starts sessions.

```sh
curl --request GET --url 'https://api.daily.co/v1/list-available-numbers?region=US' \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer $DAILY_API_KEY"

curl --request POST --url 'https://api.daily.co/v1/buy-phone-number' \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer $DAILY_API_KEY" \
  --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  --data '{"number": "+1XXXXXXXXXX"}'
```

You get back an `id` and a `number`. Keep the `id`: outbound calling wants it as
the `callerId`, not the number. `GET /v1/purchased-phone-numbers` lists them
again.

**A number cannot be released for 14 days after purchase**, and releasing is then
permanent. Plan a test number knowing you own it for at least two weeks.

### Attach the number to this agent

Point it at this agent. Two ways, same result:

- **Dashboard**: Pipecat Cloud, then Settings, then Telephony, and pick this
  agent.
- **Daily REST API**: set the domain's pinless dial-in to this agent's managed
  webhook.

```sh
curl --location 'https://api.daily.co/v1' \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer $DAILY_API_KEY" \
  --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  --data '{"properties": {"pinless_dialin": [
      {"phone_number": "<your Daily number>",
       "room_creation_api": "https://api.pipecat.daily.co/v1/public/webhooks/<org_id>/{{.Project}}/dialin"}
  ]}}'
```

`<org_id>` is your Pipecat Cloud organisation id, from the dashboard. The webhook
is the platform's own, so you do not run or host it.

Attach the number after `agent status` reports `ready`. The webhook names this
agent, so a number pointed at an agent that is not deployed yet gives the caller
silence rather than an error you can see.

### Place an outbound call

Outbound is started by the platform against this deployed agent, not by anything
in this project. Ask it to create a room with dial-out allowed and give it the
number to dial:

```sh
curl --request POST \
  --url https://api.pipecat.daily.co/v1/public/{{.Project}}/start \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer $PIPECAT_CLOUD_API_KEY" \
  --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  --data '{"createDailyRoom": true,
           "dailyRoomProperties": {"enable_dialout": true},
           "body": {"dialout_settings": [{"phoneNumber": "+1..."}]}}'
```

Two things to know before the first call:

- **The recipient sees a number you did not choose, unless you name one here.**
  With no `callerId`, the platform picks one of your purchased numbers at random.
  You can pin it in the call above by passing the purchased number's **id** (from
  `GET /v1/purchased-phone-numbers`), not the number itself:

  ```json
  "dialout_settings": [{"phoneNumber": "+1...", "callerId": "<id of a number you own>"}]
  ```

  The source package cannot declare this, so it is a choice you make per call.
  A `sipUri` works in place of `phoneNumber` for a SIP destination.
- **Dial-out has to be enabled on the domain**, and international dial-out is
  granted separately from domestic. See the prerequisites below.
